<main id="main" class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head">
    <h2>Medi-Cal</h2>
    <p class="lede">Medi-Cal is California's Medicaid program. For adults 19&ndash;64, the relevant pathway is the ACA expansion (&ldquo;MAGI New Adult Group&rdquo;): household MAGI at or below 138% FPL, with no asset test and no premium &mdash; and, through December 31, 2026, no work requirement.</p>
  </div>

  <section aria-labelledby="elig-h">
    <h3 id="elig-h">Eligibility rules</h3>
    {{TABLE:medi-cal-eligibility.csv::Medi-Cal eligibility rules (MAGI New Adult Group)}}
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="income-h">
    <h3 id="income-h">How your income is measured</h3>
    <div class="panel blue">
      <p>Medi-Cal uses <strong>MAGI</strong>: adjusted gross income from your Form 1040 plus untaxed foreign income, non-taxable Social Security benefits, and tax-exempt interest (SSI is excluded). For a trader/landlord with no wages, that means the counting unit is essentially:</p>
      <p><strong>net rental income (Schedule E) + savings interest + net capital gains from trading (after loss offsets) + any other taxable income</strong>, for the whole tax household.</p>
      <p>Rental losses and trading losses can push MAGI below the limit in bad years; gains can push it above. If Medi-Cal ends because income rose, the <a href="covered-california.html">Covered California</a> path takes over &mdash; the county or Covered California will evaluate both when you apply. Full details and sources: <a href="income-treatment.html">Income &amp; MAGI</a>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <p><strong>Illustration only &mdash; not an eligibility determination.</strong> A one-person household with $12,000 net rental income, $800 interest, and $6,000 net trading gains has MAGI of $18,800 &mdash; below the $22,025 limit (2026), so Medi-Cal would be the path; the same household with $15,000 of trading gains would be at $27,800 &mdash; above the limit, pointing to Covered California. Verify your own numbers with the county or <a href="https://www.coveredca.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="apply-h">
    <h3 id="apply-h">How to apply</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <p>Medi-Cal enrollment is open <strong>year-round</strong>. DHCS lists four ways to sign up:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Online:</strong> <a href="https://benefitscal.com/ApplyForBenefits/begin/ABOVR" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BenefitsCal</a> (apply with your county) or <a href="https://apply.coveredca.com/static/lw-web/account-creation/create" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Covered California</a> (also screens for Medi-Cal).</li>
        <li><strong>By mail:</strong> single-stream application (downloadable in many languages from the DHCS apply page).</li>
        <li><strong>By phone:</strong> call your county Medi-Cal office.</li>
        <li><strong>In person:</strong> visit a county office &mdash; <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/contact/find-a-county-office/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">find your county office</a>.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="kbd-note">Source: <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/apply/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS &mdash; Apply for Medi-Cal</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="changes-h">
    <h3 id="changes-h">What changes in 2027</h3>
    <div class="panel warn">
      <ul>
        <li><strong>January 1, 2027:</strong> the federal work and community engagement rule takes effect for the expansion adult group &mdash; see <a href="work-requirements.html">Work Requirements</a> for the six ways to satisfy it with $0 earned income.</li>
        <li><strong>January 1, 2027:</strong> retroactive coverage shrinks to one month for childless adults (two months for everyone else).</li>
        <li><strong>March 1, 2027:</strong> renewals come every six months for the expansion group. Answer county mail, texts, and emails immediately &mdash; DHCS projects most coverage losses will come from missed paperwork.</li>
        <li><strong>October 1, 2028:</strong> copayments for some adults (capped at 5% of household income; none for emergency care, checkups, pregnancy, pediatric, mental health, or community-clinic services).</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="kbd-note">Source: <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/updates/medi-cal-changes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS &mdash; Medi-Cal Changes</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="asset-h">
    <h3 id="asset-h">What about your savings and property?</h3>
    <div class="panel">
      <p>MAGI-based Medi-Cal does <strong>not</strong> count what you own. DHCS applies asset limits only to non-MAGI groups (people 65+, people with disabilities, nursing home residents, and families over the MAGI income rules). Your savings account and rental property do not count against MAGI Medi-Cal eligibility &mdash; only the <em>income</em> they generate counts. (For reference: those non-MAGI asset limits are $130,000 for one person through June 30, 2027, then $21,000 from July 1, 2027, per DHCS.)</p>
      <p class="kbd-note">Source: <a href="https://www.dhcs.ca.gov/medi-cal/updates/medi-cal-changes/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DHCS &mdash; Medi-Cal Changes (asset limits section)</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
